Spec 146 criterion 4b, which spec 250 restated 5 and 5b from and never carried
across. The screenshot was the argument: six builders and an architect at three
columns is 3 + 3 + 1, one lonely card beside two empty slots.

**Expressed as 'four columns fit', not as '1920 or wider'.** apps/client said a
viewport width because it owned the whole viewport, so available and viewport
were the same number. Here they are not: 1920 with the sidebar open is 1688px of
grid. A literal viewport threshold would offer the tile at 1920 even with the
sidebar dragged wide enough that only three columns fit — the exact ragged row 4b
exists to prevent. Four columns is not a proxy for the reason, it IS the reason:
seven items at four columns is 4 + 3, the ordinary shape of any grid.

Keyed on WIDTH and never on the builder count, which is asserted as its own test.
A count-based rule would move the architect between strip and tile as builders
come and go — a layout that reflows under a reader who did nothing.

The strip expands to a full pane and back, which is the other half of 4b: a strip
that could not expand would be hiding the architect rather than placing it. The
builders keep their grid while it is open.

With more than one architect in the workspace there is no 'the' architect, so
they all take tiles and no strip appears — a strip showing one of two would be
choosing for the reader.

Header counts agents rather than tiles: '6 builders and an architect', not '7'.

